course: everyday
lesson: 336
title: Little lies at home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Little lies at home usually start small: Owen in Leeds took two chocolate biscuits from the tin before tea and said he hadn't. Mum saw the crumbs on his jumper, and he still said no. He lost his pudding, and Mum said it was more for the lie than for the biscuits. At bedtime he owned up and said sorry for lying to her face.
explain: A lie turns a small thing into a big one. Two biscuits are nothing much; a lie told to someone's face makes them wonder what else is not true.
explain: Sometimes nobody catches you, and that can be worse. Owen's ball knocked the vase over, and he said his little sister did it. She was sent to her room crying. Nobody found out, but he could not look Mum in the eye at dinner and could not sleep.
explain: In the end he woke Mum and told her, and said sorry to his sister. On Saturday he went to confession at church: «Bless me, Father, for I have sinned.» The priest listened, gave him absolution (forgiveness in God's name) and a small penance (a short prayer or task to show he is sorry), and he walked out feeling light, the weight gone. But the priest also said: the sorry to your sister is still yours to say, and you said it — good.
explain: Lies are easy to check (a dry toothbrush, a teacher asking about chapter five, a boy looking it up), and once you are caught in a small lie, people stop trusting your big truths too.
fact: Lying about a small thing usually gets a bigger consequence than the small thing itself.
fact: Small lies are easy to check, and each one caught makes it harder for people to believe you.
fact: An excuse that is not true, like «the dog ate my homework», is a lie in a costume.
fact: Pretending to be sick does not make a fear go away; telling a grown-up what you are afraid of can.
fact: Pretending not to hear when you are called is still not doing what you were asked.
fact: Letting someone else take the blame for your wrong hurts them and leaves a heavy secret in you.
fact: A guilty conscience, like not sleeping or not looking someone in the eye, is a sign that something needs putting right.
fact: Owning up yourself, before you are caught, usually makes the trouble smaller and the trust come back faster.
fact: Confession at church can lift the weight of guilt, but it does not replace saying sorry to the person you wronged.
fact: You do not need to make up stories to be interesting; your real life is enough.
exercise: Why did Owen take the biscuits and then lie about them? | He was hungry before tea and scared of being told off | He thought the biscuits belonged to him, since they were his favourite kind | He wanted to share them with his sister | He did not know biscuits were not allowed
exercise: Owen lost his pudding after the biscuit lie. What did Mum say it was mostly for? | The lie, more than the biscuits | The crumbs left all over the kitchen floor | Eating before tea, and nothing else | Taking the last biscuits in the tin
exercise: A boy says the dog ate his homework, but his family has no dog. What is this excuse really? | A lie dressed up as an excuse | A joke that teachers always enjoy | A mistake anyone at all could make | A clever way to get more time for it
exercise: A girl tells Gran she has brushed her teeth, but the brush is dry. Why is this lie silly as well as wrong? | It is very easy to check | Gran cannot see in the dark | Teeth do not need brushing at night | Dry brushes are normal in the morning
exercise: A boy writes extra pages in his reading log that he never read. How is he most likely caught? | The teacher asks what happened in the chapters | His friends tell the teacher at once, because they saw him | The pages in the log change colour | The book has a counter inside it
exercise: A boy tells friends his dad is a famous footballer. One looks it up. What usually happens next? | His friends stop believing him, even about true things | His friends think he is even cooler and want to meet his dad | Nothing, because nobody minds fibs | His dad becomes a footballer
exercise: A girl pretends to be sick to miss swimming because the deep end scares her. What would put it right? | Telling Mum she is scared, so the teacher can help | Saying the pool water was dirty and made her feel ill | Pretending to be sick again next week | Staying in bed every swimming day
exercise: Why does a pretend illness not solve the fear of the deep end? | The fear is still there next week | Being in bed makes you a better swimmer | Pools close when someone in the class is ill | Pretending makes the fear disappear
exercise: A boy says he didn't hear Mum call him, but she saw him look up. What is the honest way next time? | Answer the first call, even if he is busy | Wear headphones so he really can't hear her at all | Wait for the third call to answer | Say he was asleep
exercise: Owen let his sister take the blame for the vase. Nobody found out. How did he feel? | Guilty: he could not sleep or look Mum in the eye | Happy and proud, because he had got away with it | Proud of his clever story | Nothing at all, since nobody knew
exercise: What did Owen do to put the vase lie right? | Woke Mum, told her the truth and said sorry to his sister | Bought his sister a sweet the next day and said nothing about it | Promised himself never to play ball again | Waited for his sister to forget about it
exercise: After confession, Owen felt light again. What did the priest say was still his job? | Saying sorry to his sister, the person he wronged | Nothing more, because absolution fixes everything at once | Telling the whole school what he did | Never playing with a ball again
exercise: Which choice usually makes a small wrong smaller? | Owning up quickly, before you are caught | Waiting a few days to see if anyone notices | Blaming someone younger | Telling the lie more firmly
exercise: Why do grown-ups get crosser about the lie than about the biscuits? | The lie breaks their trust, which matters more than biscuits | Biscuits are very expensive and they were saving them for guests | They like to punish children | Lying is only bad on school days
exercise: A boy hid his PE kit to miss running in the cold, and it fell out of his bag. What did he lose by lying? | His teacher's trust, and he had to run anyway | Nothing, because PE was cancelled that day anyway | His kit, forever | Only the chance to run in the cold
exercise: What is the wise choice when you are tempted to make up a story to sound interesting? | Tell something true about yourself | Make the story even bigger and funnier | Say nothing to anyone, ever again, at all | Copy a story you saw on the TV last night
test: bro says ur at Tom's every day but Tom's mum just texted ours lol. what was the lie for, and what's the fix | He wanted more time out; tell the truth and ask properly next time | He should ask Tom to cover for him next time so the mums don't find out | Nothing, Tom's mum is wrong | He should stop going out at all
test: Mum's text: «found the vase. your sister says it wasn't her. anything u want to tell me??» What is the wise reply? | Tell her it was your ball and say sorry to your sister | Say nothing at all and hope it all blows over by tomorrow | Say the cat did it | Say your sister is lying
test: «dog ate it, miss» «Jake you don't HAVE a dog». What is the best thing for Jake to do now? | Say sorry, admit he didn't do it and do the work | Say it was the neighbour's dog, it gets in the garden | Say the wind blew it away | Cry so the teacher feels bad
test: Kid in the playground: «i said i was sick for swimming twice and it worked, so it's fine». What is wrong with that? | It worked twice, but it is still a lie and the fear stays | Nothing is wrong with it, because if a trick works it is fine | He should do it only once a month | It is only wrong if Mum finds out
test: Sister: «I'm tellin Mum u never brushed, the brush is bone dry». What is the honest next step? | Go and brush them and tell Gran the truth | Wet the brush quickly under the tap | Say the brush dries fast | Say the sister is lying
test: Father at confession: «Is there anyone you need to make it right with?» Owen lied to his mum about biscuits. What is the truthful answer? | Yes, Mum, and he has said sorry to her | No, confession sorts out everything | Only the biscuit tin | Nobody, because it was only two biscuits
test: Mate: «just say u read it, nobody checks reading logs». Why is that wrong advice? | Teachers do ask about the book, and the lie gets found | Reading logs are checked by computers, so it's the computer's job | Lying in a log is allowed | Books know when you skip pages
test: Dad, annoyed: «I called you three times. Don't tell me you didn't hear.» What is the honest answer? | «I heard, I didn't want to stop my game. Sorry» | «My ears were blocked, I've got a cold» | «You must have called someone else» | «I was asleep with my eyes open»
test: A boy owns up to a lie at bedtime, before Mum asks. What usually follows? | The trouble is smaller and trust comes back faster | He gets a much bigger punishment | Mum stops believing anything he says | Nothing changes at all
test: Which is TRUE about lying to get out of trouble? | It often makes the trouble bigger when it comes out | It always works if the story is good and you keep it the same | It is fine if nobody gets hurt at once | Grown-ups never notice small lies
test: Friend, sneering: «your dad's NOT a footballer, liar». What fixes it best? | Admit he made it up to seem interesting and stop doing it | Say the dad plays in secret | Get angry and push the friend | Make up a bigger story
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a parent may impose a sanction more severe for a falsehood than for the minor offence it was intended to conceal. | The falsehood undermines the trust upon which the relationship depends | Parents are obliged by law to punish all falsehoods more severely than other faults | The minor offence is invariably of great financial consequence | The falsehood is of no consequence whatsoever
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a child who permits a sibling to bear the blame for his own misconduct. | He wrongs the sibling and is commonly burdened by a troubled conscience | He commits no wrong provided the matter remains undiscovered | He thereby transfers the fault lawfully to the sibling | He is relieved of any obligation to make amends
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relation between sacramental confession and restitution to the person wronged? | Confession may relieve the burden of guilt but does not replace apology and restitution | Confession entirely removes any duty to apologise or to make amends to the person wronged | Restitution is required only where confession is refused | Neither confession nor apology has any bearing upon the matter
exam: Determine which course of action is most likely to diminish the consequences of a minor falsehood. | A voluntary admission made promptly, prior to discovery | A more elaborate repetition of the original falsehood | The attribution of the fault to a younger child | The avoidance of the person deceived for some days
